{"shard": 9, "documents": [{"id": "1053", "title": "Liquidity surge fuels speculative rally in low-cap stocks", "url": "https://businessday.ng/markets/article/liquidity-surge-fuels-speculative-rally-in-low-cap-stocks/", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/markets/article/liquidity-surge-fuels-speculative-rally-in-low-cap-stocks/", "text": "Share Advertisement In this article [First Holdco (+233.82%), Zenith Bank (+116.83 %) led financial services and banking](https://businessday.ng/markets/article/liquidity-surge-fuels-speculative-rally-in-low-cap-stocks/#first-holdco-233-82-zenith-bank-116-83-led-financial-services-and-banking) [ZICHIS (+900%), SCOA (+365.49 %) led Industrial Goods, Construction and Agribusiness](https://businessday.ng/markets/article/liquidity-surge-fuels-speculative-rally-in-low-cap-stocks/#zichis-900-scoa-365-49-led-industrial-goods-construction-and-agribusiness) [Oil & Gas, ICT, Services and Conglomerates](https://businessday.ng/markets/article/liquidity-surge-fuels-speculative-rally-in-low-cap-stocks/#oil-gas-ict-services-and-conglomerates) As the Nigerian stock market closed out the first three quarters of 2026, a powerful wave of capital reshaped market dynamics. Rather than remaining concentrated in mega-cap safe havens, excess liquidity actively spilt over into the market\u2019s most dynamic corners. This fresh wave of funding ignited sharp, speculative rallies across low-cap equities, catching the attention of traders and investors alike. For many active investors, the liquidity shift during the nine months leading up to September 30 presented a high-stakes window of opportunity\u2014and a critical test of risk management as valuations outpaced traditional fundamentals. Interestingly, while heavyweight blue-chip stocks and traditional market bellwethers typically command the spotlight, the past nine months inverted conventional wisdom. Agile, lesser-known smaller companies across the agro-allied, industrial, and service sectors surged past their large-cap counterparts, delivering phenomenal rallies. This remarkable outperformance signals a profound structural shift in investor appetite: moving away from safety-first stagnation toward aggressive growth, tactical turnaround plays, and an unyielding hunt for alpha in Nigeria\u2019s vibrant financial ecosystem. Read also: [Dangote to list 10% of Ethiopian fertiliser business on Africa\u2019s youngest stock exchange](https://businessday.ng/africa/article/dangote-to-list-10-of-ethiopian-fertiliser-business-on-africas-youngest-stock-exchange/) BusinessDay\u2019s analytical breakdown of 138 stocks for the nine months ending in September reveals 91 gainers, 46 losers, and 1 flat stock, highlighting a broad-based yet highly selective bull market structure. The most striking feature of the nine-month data is the extreme performance of specific low-cap equities\u2014led by Zichis (+900 percent), Fortis Global Insurance (+805 percent), and SCOA (+365.49 percent). This illustrates a market environment where liquidity cascades into low-free-float stocks, creating speculative rewards for agile, risk-on portfolios. At the same time, Tier-1 banking counters demonstrated strong defensive resilience. Core financial institutions like First Holdco (+233.82 percent) and Zenith Bank (+116.83 percent) proved that fundamental earnings strength and robust balance sheet optimisation could comfortably outpace inflation. Conversely, high-profile consumer goods manufacturers such as Nestl\u00e9 Nigeria (-48.11 percent) and Austinlaz (-48.24 percent) underscored the severe impact of foreign exchange volatility, high borrowing costs, and compressed consumer purchasing power on corporate bottom lines. In the first nine months of 2026, the Nigerian Exchange Limited All-Share Index (NGX-ASI) recorded a year-to-date (YtD) return of 61.43 percent. The index rose significantly, closing at 251,211.67 basis points on September 30, 2026, as against 153,539.83 basis points at the beginning of the year. Also, total market capitalisation surged by approximately N63.73 trillion (64.13 percent increase), moving from N99.376 trillion at the start of the year to close the nine-month period at N163.105 trillion. Read also: [Africa\u2019s oldest stock exchange eyes Dangote Group shares through depositary receipts](https://businessday.ng/africa/article/africas-oldest-stock-exchange-eyes-dangote-group-shares-through-depositary-receipts/) First Holdco (+233.82%), Zenith Bank (+116.83 %) led financial services and banking The banking and financial services sector remained a primary institutional battleground, characterised by stellar Tier-1 resilience and aggressive rallies among select financial holding companies. Gainers: First Holdco (+233.82 percent), Zenith Bank (+116.83 percent), Jaizbank (+78.02 percent), Stanbic (+63 percent), ETI (+62.29 percent), Custodian (+62.79 percent), Wema Bank (+54.41 percent), Consolidated Hallmark (+64.06 percent), GTCO (+45.53 percent), Access Holdings (+44.76 percent), Fidelity Bank (+13.95 percent), UBA (+9.12 percent), Abbey Bank (+9.38 percent), NEM Insurance (+7.09 percent), Mutual Benefit (+0.97 percent), AIICO (+0.26 percent), Critical Minerals Financing Corporation (+127.37 percent), Fortis Global Insurance (+805 percent), NGX Group (+158.57 percent), NPF Microfinance Bank (+18.60 percent), and VfD Group (+31.82 percent). Losers: FCMB (-4.98 percent), Cornestone Insurance (-6.04 percent), Guinea Insurance (-47.37 percent), LASACO (-27.76 percent), Linkage Assurance (-4.49 percent), AXA Mansard (-19.71 percent), Prestige Assurance (-12.66percent), Regency Alliance Insurance (-35.19 percent), Royal Exchange (-38.17 percent), Sovereign Trust Insurance (-42.41 percent), Sunu Assurances (-44 percent), Coronation Insurance (-27.38%), Veritas Kapital Assurance (-35.09 percent), Africa Prudential (-26.01 percent), AVA Capital (-24.67 percent), Living Trust Mortgage Bank (-8.99 percent), United Capital (-9.09 percent). ZICHIS (+900%), SCOA (+365.49 %) led Industrial Goods, Construction and Agribusiness Heavyweights and agricultural plays displayed massive value expansion, driven by infrastructure demand and defensive positioning. Gainers: ZICHIS (+900 percent), SCOA (+365.49 percent), Berger Paints (+207.50 percent), Union Dicon (+244.20 percent), HBM Nigeria (+163.94 percent), Dangote Cement (+75.16 percent), BUA Cement (+66.39 percent), CAP (+44.93 percent), Beta Glass (+38.24 percent), Meyer (+23.17 percent), Vitafoam (+110.87 percent), NASCON (+75.72 percent), PZ Cussons (+66.18 percent), Unilever (+45.69 percent), Dangote Sugar (+13.33 percent), and Guiness Nigeria (+9.97 percent). Other gainers are: Nigerian Breweries (+0.93 percent), Nigerian Enamelware (+0.49 percent), FTN Cocoa (+66 percent), Livestock Feeds (+33.88 percent), Okomu Oil Palm (+16.55 percent), Presco (+41.06 percent), UACN (+98.90 percent), Julius Berger (+103.27 percent), Chapel Hill Denham Nigeria Infrastructure Debt Fund (+28.43 percent), Fidson Healthcare (+69.66 percent), May & Baker (+106.32 percent), and Neimeth (+32.76 percent). Read also: [Here are six African countries where investors can access the Dangote IPO](https://businessday.ng/africa/article/here-are-six-african-countries-where-investors-can-access-the-dangote-ipo/) Other major advancers in this sector are: NCR (+121.73 percent), Omatek (+28.32 percent), Multiverse (+43.82 percent), ABC Transport (+64.63 percent), Eunisell (+64.35 percent), Ikeja Hotel (+6.21 percent), Learn Africa (+16.79 percent), NAHCO (+38.89 percent), RedStar Express (+81.61 percent), RT Briscoe (+185.71 percent), Skyway Aviation Handling Company (+93.56 percent), Tantalizers (+56 percent), The Initiates (+104.51 percent), Trans Nationwide Express (+25.58 percent), and Transcorp Hotels (+69.98 percent). Losers: Austinlaz (-48.24percent), Cutix (-25.81percent), BUA Foods (-4.79 percent), Cadbury (-0.67percent), Champion (-29.29 percent), Honeywell Flourmills (-17.81 percent), Nestle (-48.11 percent), Northern Nigeria Flourmill (-5.81 percent), Ellahlakes (-38.06 percent), Chellaram (-26.52 percent), Transcorp (-18.50 percent), Haldane McCall (-14.75 percent), UPDC (-23.47 percent), and Mecure (-13.80 percent). Others are: Chams (-18.21 percent), Etranzact (+7.49 percent, Secure Electronic Technology (-9.21 percent), Trippleg (-47.29 percent), Legend Internet (-31 percent), IMG Nigeria (-12.50 percent), Transcorp Power (-42.02 percent), Academy Press (-30.14 percent), Caverton (-25 percent), C &I leasing (-19.71 percent), UPL (-21.67 percent). Oil & Gas, ICT, Services and Conglomerates Mixed operational realities defined the energy and technology space, balancing global commodity price shifts with domestic volume constraints. Gainers: Seplat (+175.44 percent), Eterna (+54.39 percent), Japaul Gold (+25.54 percent), Conoil (+12.18 percent), CWG (+15.28 percent), Airtel Africa (+177.53 percent), Daarcomm (+61.29 percent), MTNN (+63.80 percent). Losers: Oando (-12.94 percent), Total (-19 percent), Geregu (-27.67 percent). Related News [EPL: Man City beat Ipswich, Liverpool survive Brentford scare](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/epl-man-city-beat-ipswich-liverpool-survive-brentford-scare/) [Iwobi\u2019s goal seals Fulham\u2019s first Premier League win](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/iwobis-goal-seals-fulhams-first-premier-league-win/) [World Bank report: ADC asks Tinubu to explain missing $12.4bn](https://businessday.ng/news/article/world-bank-report-adc-asks-tinubu-to-explain-missing-12-4bn/) GameCenter Widget: businessday.ng [Iheanyi Nwachukwu](https://businessday.ng/author/iheanyi-nwachukwu/) Iheanyi Nwachukwu, is a creative content writer with over two decades journalism experience writing on banking, finance, capital markets, and tax. The multiple awards winning journalist is Deputy Editor, BusinessDay. Iheanyi holds BSc Degree in Economics from Imo State University; Master of Science (MSc) Degree in Management from University of Lagos. Iheanyi has attended several work-related trainings including (i) Advanced Writing and Reporting Skills (Pan African University, Lagos); (ii) News Agency Journalism (Indian Institute of Mass Communication {IIMC}, New Delhi, India); and (iii) Capital Markets Development and Regulations (International Law Institute {ILI} of Georgetown University, Washington DC, USA). Other trainings Iheanyi attended include: Economic/Political Risk Analysis (By Thomson Reuters Foundation); International Financial Journalism (IFJ) (By PMA Media Training, UK); Effective Business Writing Skills (By Phillips Consulting); Reporting on Corporate Governance (By International Finance Corporation (IFC) & Thomson Reuters Foundation UK); etc. In addition, he has participated in high-level economy & markets events in Dubai, South Africa, Morocco, and other African countries like Zambia, Ghana and Gambia. Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [NGX Group, partners advance cross-border investment collaboration through Dangote Refinery offer in Egypt, Ethiopia](https://businessday.ng/markets/article/ngx-group-partners-advance-cross-border-investment-collaboration-through-dangote-refinery-offer-in-egypt-ethiopia/) [Dangote Group, Botswana Exchange laud NGX Group for driving cross-border collaboration](https://businessday.ng/markets/article/dangote-group-botswana-exchange-laud-ngx-group-for-driving-cross-border-collaboration/) [What an IPO extension does to the Exchange](https://businessday.ng/markets/article/what-an-ipo-extension-does-to-the-exchange/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "5c57654e43530950d8d620ad135e72556d4c091b140e25ba08641a90a93c1e94", "domain": "businessday", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:30:07Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1054", "title": "NGX Group, partners advance cross-border investment collaboration through Dangote Refinery offer in Egypt, Ethiopia", "url": "https://businessday.ng/markets/article/ngx-group-partners-advance-cross-border-investment-collaboration-through-dangote-refinery-offer-in-egypt-ethiopia/", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/markets/article/ngx-group-partners-advance-cross-border-investment-collaboration-through-dangote-refinery-offer-in-egypt-ethiopia/", "text": "Share Advertisement Nigerian Exchange Group Plc (NGX Group) and its partners are advancing cross-border investment collaboration among African capital markets, with engagements in Cairo, Egypt, and Addis Ababa, Ethiopia, exploring how market infrastructure and institutional partnerships can improve investment access across the continent. Using the Dangote Petroleum Refinery and Petrochemicals FZE offer as a practical context, the discussions focused on connecting investors across jurisdictions and mobilising African capital for industrial development. Held on Thursday, October 15, 2026, the engagements were hosted in Cairo by the Egyptian Exchange (EGX), in collaboration with the African Export-Import Bank (Afreximbank), and in Addis Ababa by the Ethiopian Securities Exchange (ESX). They brought together exchange leaders, financial institutions and other capital market stakeholders to explore practical approaches to improving market connectivity, broadening investor participation and mobilising African capital for productive investment. Read also: [Dangote Group, Botswana Exchange laud NGX Group for driving cross-border collaboration](https://businessday.ng/markets/article/dangote-group-botswana-exchange-laud-ngx-group-for-driving-cross-border-collaboration/) Speaking during the discussions, Temi Popoola, Group Managing Director/Chief Executive Officer of NGX Group, emphasised the importance of addressing longstanding structural barriers that limit investment flows across African markets. He noted that the continent possesses substantial pools of institutional capital, but stronger market infrastructure and cooperation are needed to connect these resources with investment opportunities across national borders. \u201cAfrica has substantial capital within its own markets, particularly across pension funds and insurance companies. The challenge is how to connect that capital to investment opportunities across the continent. The Dangote Refinery offer provides a practical opportunity to address some of the infrastructure barriers to cross-border investment. Beyond a single transaction, it is about creating connections that enable African capital to finance African growth,\u201d Popoola said. Aliko Dangote, President and Chief Executive of Dangote Industries Limited, highlighted the refinery as an example of the scale of industrial investment required to strengthen Africa\u2019s productive capacity, reduce dependence on imports and support long-term economic growth. He emphasised the importance of developing stronger channels through which African capital can support major industrial projects across the continent. He also commended NGX Group for its role in bringing African exchanges together around the initiative and acknowledged the participating exchanges for their collaboration. Umaru Kwairanga, Group Chairman of NGX Group, stressed that lasting progress would depend on sustained institutional cooperation and the development of market infrastructure that makes cross-border investment more accessible and efficient. \u201cOur task is not simply to collaborate around one offer, but to develop lasting partnerships that support cross-border investment and capital formation across Africa. This requires cooperation among exchanges, regulators, financial institutions and businesses, alongside investment in the infrastructure and technology that make participation more accessible,\u201d Kwairanga said. Read also: [Why Nigeria\u2019s fintech giants keep giving the NGX a wide berth](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/why-nigerias-fintech-giants-keep-giving-the-ngx-a-wide-berth/) At the Cairo engagement, Omar Radwan, Executive Chairman of the Egyptian Exchange, highlighted the potential for instruments such as Egyptian Depositary Receipts (EDRs) to provide a channel through which Egyptian investors could access selected African businesses. He expressed EGX\u2019s readiness to work with Dangote Industries to explore a possible framework for such an arrangement. George Elombi, President and Chairman of Afreximbank, reaffirmed the bank\u2019s longstanding financing relationship with Dangote Industries and its commitment to supporting African industrial development. He highlighted the importance of mobilising African investment towards enterprises capable of contributing to the continent\u2019s economic transformation. In Addis Ababa, discussions focused on expanding investment opportunities for Ethiopian investors and strengthening Ethiopia\u2019s participation in Africa\u2019s evolving capital market landscape. Yodit Kassa, Chief Executive Officer of ESX, welcomed the engagement, noting its significance for Ethiopian investors and the country\u2019s growing capital market. \u201cThe Dangote Petroleum Refinery offer represents an important opportunity for Ethiopian investors to participate in the growth of one of Africa\u2019s leading industrial enterprises. It also reflects the growing confidence in Ethiopia\u2019s capital market and our readiness to work with leading institutions across the continent,\u201d Kassa said. Read also: [Why Nigeria\u2019s fintech giants keep giving the NGX a wide berth](https://businessday.ng/business-economy/article/why-nigerias-fintech-giants-keep-giving-the-ngx-a-wide-berth/) The Cairo and Addis Ababa engagements followed earlier stops at the Nairobi Securities Exchange and the Botswana Stock Exchange, extending discussions on cross-border investment across multiple African markets. The discussions also highlighted the practical requirements for cross-border participation, including regulatory recognition, distribution, investor verification, settlement, custody and access to reliable information. The offer provides a real-world opportunity for exchanges, regulators, intermediaries and financial technology platforms to address these considerations and explore workable solutions within their respective regulatory frameworks. For participating markets, it also raises the question of how local savings can be channelled more efficiently into productive enterprises across the continent. NGX Group remains committed to working with partner exchanges and financial institutions to strengthen market infrastructure, expand cross-border investment access and advance collaboration among African capital markets, contributing to the continent\u2019s long-term economic and industrial development. Related News [EPL: Man City beat Ipswich, Liverpool survive Brentford scare](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/epl-man-city-beat-ipswich-liverpool-survive-brentford-scare/) [Iwobi\u2019s goal seals Fulham\u2019s first Premier League win](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/iwobis-goal-seals-fulhams-first-premier-league-win/) [World Bank report: ADC asks Tinubu to explain missing $12.4bn](https://businessday.ng/news/article/world-bank-report-adc-asks-tinubu-to-explain-missing-12-4bn/) GameCenter Widget: businessday.ng [Iheanyi Nwachukwu](https://businessday.ng/author/iheanyi-nwachukwu/) Iheanyi Nwachukwu, is a creative content writer with over two decades journalism experience writing on banking, finance, capital markets, and tax. The multiple awards winning journalist is Deputy Editor, BusinessDay. Iheanyi holds BSc Degree in Economics from Imo State University; Master of Science (MSc) Degree in Management from University of Lagos. Iheanyi has attended several work-related trainings including (i) Advanced Writing and Reporting Skills (Pan African University, Lagos); (ii) News Agency Journalism (Indian Institute of Mass Communication {IIMC}, New Delhi, India); and (iii) Capital Markets Development and Regulations (International Law Institute {ILI} of Georgetown University, Washington DC, USA). Other trainings Iheanyi attended include: Economic/Political Risk Analysis (By Thomson Reuters Foundation); International Financial Journalism (IFJ) (By PMA Media Training, UK); Effective Business Writing Skills (By Phillips Consulting); Reporting on Corporate Governance (By International Finance Corporation (IFC) & Thomson Reuters Foundation UK); etc. In addition, he has participated in high-level economy & markets events in Dubai, South Africa, Morocco, and other African countries like Zambia, Ghana and Gambia. Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [Liquidity surge fuels speculative rally in low-cap stocks](https://businessday.ng/markets/article/liquidity-surge-fuels-speculative-rally-in-low-cap-stocks/) [Dangote Group, Botswana Exchange laud NGX Group for driving cross-border collaboration](https://businessday.ng/markets/article/dangote-group-botswana-exchange-laud-ngx-group-for-driving-cross-border-collaboration/) [What an IPO extension does to the Exchange](https://businessday.ng/markets/article/what-an-ipo-extension-does-to-the-exchange/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "d75d9893f5b9e63a8a8682e9c78a18d5f0ad2f63127c05457e76e47766a5d0a1", "domain": "businessday", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:30:08Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1055", "title": "Dangote Group, Botswana Exchange laud NGX Group for driving cross-border collaboration", "url": "https://businessday.ng/markets/article/dangote-group-botswana-exchange-laud-ngx-group-for-driving-cross-border-collaboration/", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/markets/article/dangote-group-botswana-exchange-laud-ngx-group-for-driving-cross-border-collaboration/", "text": "Share Advertisement Dangote Industries Limited and the Botswana Stock Exchange (BSE) have commended Nigerian Exchange Group (NGX Group) for bringing African exchanges together to expand cross-border investment opportunities through the ongoing roadshow for the Dangote Petroleum Refinery and Petrochemicals FZE offer. The commendation came on Wednesday at an investor engagement in Gaborone, following an earlier engagement in Nairobi, Kenya. The roadshow forms part of NGX Group\u2019s wider initiative to deepen collaboration among African exchanges and widen access to investment opportunities across the continent. The roadshow continues with engagements at the Egyptian Exchange (EGX) and the Ethiopian Securities Exchange (ESX), extending the initiative\u2019s reach and creating further opportunities for African exchanges to collaborate on cross-border investment. Read also: [Botswana to host Southern African trading hub for Dangote refinery IPO](https://businessday.ng/africa/article/botswana-to-host-southern-african-trading-hub-for-dangote-refinery-ipo/) Murat Erden, Group Chief Financial Officer of Dangote Industries Limited, praised NGX Group, under the leadership of Group Chairman, Umaru Kwairanga and Group Managing Director/Chief Executive Officer, Temi Popoola, for galvanising African exchanges around the initiative, which began with an engagement convened by NGX Group in Lagos in April 2026. He also highlighted the role of digital innovation in broadening access to the offer, citing the addition of WhatsApp to NGX Invest, and acknowledged the partnership between NGX Group and participating exchanges in reaching investors across their respective markets. Aobakwe Aupa Monyatsi, Chief Executive Officer of the BSE, also welcomed the collaboration, highlighting the value of partnerships among African exchanges and the opportunity to widen participation by encouraging additional markets to join the initiative. At the earlier engagement in Nairobi, Aliko Dangote, president and Chief Executive of Dangote Industries Limited, lauded Kwairanga and Popoola for their role in advancing collaboration among African capital-market institutions. He highlighted how stronger ties between exchanges could enable companies to raise capital across multiple markets rather than being limited to their home markets. Citing the planned refinery in Mokowe-Lamu, Kenya, as an example, he pointed to the potential for businesses operating across Africa to list in more than one market. Umaru Kwairanga, chairman, NGX Group said: \u201cOur vision is to build stronger connections among African capital markets, making it easier for businesses to access capital and for investors to participate in opportunities across the continent. The collaboration around the Dangote Refinery offer demonstrates what African exchanges can achieve when we work together towards a shared objective.\u201d Temi Popoola, Group Managing Director/Chief Executive Officer, NGX Group has similarly emphasised the importance of translating continental ambition into practical partnerships. He said: \u201cWhen we began this engagement, our objective was continental: to bring African exchanges together and explore how we can create stronger connections between African capital markets.\u201d NGX Invest, NGX Group\u2019s electronic public-offering platform, supports subscriptions through more than 180 distribution channels, including stockbrokers, banks and fintech companies. The addition of WhatsApp as an access channel further expands the ways investors can access the offer. Related News [Odunaike kicks off Lagos governorship campaign, vows to fix state](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/odunaike-kicks-off-lagos-governorship-campaign-vows-to-fix-state/) [EPL: Man City beat Ipswich, Liverpool survive Brentford scare](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/epl-man-city-beat-ipswich-liverpool-survive-brentford-scare/) [Obi-Kwankwaso movement salutes music warriors fueling new Nigeria](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/obi-kwankwaso-movement-salutes-music-warriors-fueling-new-nigeria/) GameCenter Widget: businessday.ng [Iheanyi Nwachukwu](https://businessday.ng/author/iheanyi-nwachukwu/) Iheanyi Nwachukwu, is a creative content writer with over two decades journalism experience writing on banking, finance, capital markets, and tax. The multiple awards winning journalist is Deputy Editor, BusinessDay. Iheanyi holds BSc Degree in Economics from Imo State University; Master of Science (MSc) Degree in Management from University of Lagos. Iheanyi has attended several work-related trainings including (i) Advanced Writing and Reporting Skills (Pan African University, Lagos); (ii) News Agency Journalism (Indian Institute of Mass Communication {IIMC}, New Delhi, India); and (iii) Capital Markets Development and Regulations (International Law Institute {ILI} of Georgetown University, Washington DC, USA). Other trainings Iheanyi attended include: Economic/Political Risk Analysis (By Thomson Reuters Foundation); International Financial Journalism (IFJ) (By PMA Media Training, UK); Effective Business Writing Skills (By Phillips Consulting); Reporting on Corporate Governance (By International Finance Corporation (IFC) & Thomson Reuters Foundation UK); etc. In addition, he has participated in high-level economy & markets events in Dubai, South Africa, Morocco, and other African countries like Zambia, Ghana and Gambia. Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [Angola\u2019s fuel subsidies spending projected to hit $4 billion in 2026, official says](https://businessday.ng/africa/article/angolas-fuel-subsidies-spending-projected-to-hit-4-billion-in-2026-official-says/) [West African investors identify $10bn in projects ahead of regional investment summit](https://businessday.ng/africa/article/west-african-investors-identify-10bn-in-projects-ahead-of-regional-investment-summit/) [Here are six African countries extending Dangote IPO beyond Nigeria](https://businessday.ng/africa/article/here-are-six-african-countries-where-investors-can-access-the-dangote-ipo/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "c8f9ec70929694431882d9f15e3c534a6e59bc296d735ddb23ec9edebf9d3f7f", "domain": "businessday", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:30:09Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1056", "title": "What an IPO extension does to the Exchange", "url": "https://businessday.ng/markets/article/what-an-ipo-extension-does-to-the-exchange/", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/markets/article/what-an-ipo-extension-does-to-the-exchange/", "text": "Share Advertisement In the fast-paced world of capital markets, timing isn\u2019t just a detail on a calendar it\u2019s millions of dollars on the table. When news broke that the highly anticipated Dangote Refinery Initial Public Offering (IPO) window would be adjusted by two weeks, a silent ripple moved through trading desks across the Nigerian Exchange. Across Lagos, retail traders, fund managers, and institutional desks had spent weeks liquidating positions, pulling cash out of secondary market equities, and building up war chests to bid for shares in Africa\u2019s industrial crown jewel. But when an IPO timeline shifts, that capital doesn\u2019t disappear. To understand why a two-week delay creates such waves, you have to look at how retail and institutional investors prepare for a massive listing. When a corporate titan prepares to go public, it acts as a liquidity magnet. Institutional investors rebalance their portfolios, dumping lower-yielding holdings to create space for the new asset. Retail investors hold off on fresh equity purchases, stashing their capital in low-yield money market funds or brokerage cash accounts, ready to pounce when the second book-building opens. When the offer date gets pushed back, that pooled liquidity stays frozen on the sidelines. The secondary market experiences a temporary \u201cliquidity vacuum\u201d trading volumes drop on standard equities, bid-ask spreads widen, and middle-tier stocks experience artificial price stagnation simply because the market\u2019s money is sitting in a waiting room. Historical Precedents, When Global and Local Giants Paused\u2026 A timing adjustment on an IPO is far from unprecedented; in fact, some of the world\u2019s most successful market debuts involved strategic timeline shifts to optimise market conditions and protect valuations Saudi Aramco (2019): The largest initial public offering in history was delayed multiple times over two years. The issuing houses paused the debut to restructure corporate governance, finalise international book-building allocations, and wait for favorable crude oil price stabilisation. When it finally hit the market, the pause paid off, securing a record-breaking two trillion-dollar valuation on its opening day. MTN Nigeria (2019): Before its landmark listing on the Nigerian Stock Exchange, the telecoms giant faced timeline shifts while navigating complex regulatory clearances, tax reconciliations, and capital structure approvals with the Securities and Exchange Commission and the Central Bank of Nigeria. The extra preparation time ensured the listing by introduction was seamless and oversubscribed. Ant Group (2020): On the extreme end of the spectrum, the fintech giant\u2019s multi-billion-dollar dual listing in Shanghai and Hong Kong was halted just days before its debut due to sudden regulatory framework shifts regarding capital reserves. While dramatic, it highlighted how regulatory alignment overrides initial calendar deadlines. In almost every historical case, a brief pause is driven by three core strategic drivers, optimising the order book to secure heavy foreign institutional allocations, navigating regulatory fine print to ensure 100 percent compliance, or waiting out macro-volatility like interest rate announcements. When you relate these historical lessons to the current two-week adjustment, three distinct market dynamics emerge on the trading floor. 1. The Secondary Market Rebound With primary offer cash temporarily locked up for an extra fourteen days, short-term day traders and yield-hungry investors often execute \u201cinterim trades.\u201d Instead of letting cash sit idle in non-interest-yielding brokerage accounts, liquidity briefly flows back into high-dividend tier-one banking shares or liquid consumer goods stocks to capture short-term price swings. 2. The Institutional Order Book Polish For issuing houses and lead underwriters, a two-week extension is rarely a setback it is a war room opportunity. It provides investment bankers two extra weeks of international roadshows, allowing them to convert non-binding expressions of interest from foreign portfolio investors into firm, multi-million-dollar institutional commitments. 3. The Retail Investor Dilemma For everyday retail investors, a delay tests patience and discipline. The temptation during an offer extension is to pull cash out and chase short-term momentum stocks. However, disciplined market participants use the extra two weeks to do what every smart investor should do: thoroughly analyse the red-herring prospectus, evaluate debt-to-equity ratios, and calculate long-term dividend yield expectations. A two-week adjustment in a massive public offer is not a sign of weakness, it is a tactical calibration in a high stakes financial game. For the Nigerian Exchange, the temporary freeze in liquidity is simply the calm before the storm. As the offer window remains open, that accumulated, waiting capital will flood back into the market proving that in capital markets, patience is often the most profitable strategy. Related News [EPL: Man City beat Ipswich, Liverpool survive Brentford scare](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/epl-man-city-beat-ipswich-liverpool-survive-brentford-scare/) [Iwobi\u2019s goal seals Fulham\u2019s first Premier League win](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/iwobis-goal-seals-fulhams-first-premier-league-win/) [World Bank report: ADC asks Tinubu to explain missing $12.4bn](https://businessday.ng/news/article/world-bank-report-adc-asks-tinubu-to-explain-missing-12-4bn/) GameCenter Widget: businessday.ng Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [Liquidity surge fuels speculative rally in low-cap stocks](https://businessday.ng/markets/article/liquidity-surge-fuels-speculative-rally-in-low-cap-stocks/) [NGX Group, partners advance cross-border investment collaboration through Dangote Refinery offer in Egypt, Ethiopia](https://businessday.ng/markets/article/ngx-group-partners-advance-cross-border-investment-collaboration-through-dangote-refinery-offer-in-egypt-ethiopia/) [Dangote Group, Botswana Exchange laud NGX Group for driving cross-border collaboration](https://businessday.ng/markets/article/dangote-group-botswana-exchange-laud-ngx-group-for-driving-cross-border-collaboration/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "939ec26fbb3caa0625b34a99ab4476df222185208771c1d884d2cf89a7093559", "domain": "businessday", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:30:10Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1057", "title": "Nigeria\u2019s SEC flags Wayvepay over suspected investment fraud", "url": "https://businessday.ng/markets/article/nigerias-sec-flags-wayvepay-over-suspected-investment-fraud/", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/markets/article/nigerias-sec-flags-wayvepay-over-suspected-investment-fraud/", "text": "Share Advertisement The Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) has issued a stern warning to the investing public regarding the suspected fraudulent activities of WayvePay, clarifying that the online investment platform is entirely unregistered and unauthorised to operate within the Nigerian capital market. Highlighting the severe risks of dealing with unregulated entities, the regulator has advised investors to steer clear of the platform to protect themselves against potential financial fraud and the total loss of their funds. In its recent public notice, the SEC said its attention has been drawn to the activities of \u201can online investment platform known as WAYVEPAY, which operates through its website as well as various social media platforms and a Mobile App.\u201d \u201cThe Commission hereby informs the public that WAYVEPAY is not registered by the Commission to solicit investments from the public or operate in any capacity within the Nigerian capital market. \u201cAccordingly, the public is advised to refrain from investing with the said WAYVEPAY or any of its representative/agent in respect of any business relating to the Nigerian capital market, as any person who engages with the entity or its representatives does so at their own risk\u201d, SEC stated. The Commission reiterated that transacting in the Nigerian Capital Market with unregistered and unregulated entities exposes investors to the risk of fraud and potential loss of investment. \u201cThe public is therefore advised to verify the registration status of companies and entities offering investment opportunities through the Commission\u2019s dedicated portal,\u201d SEC said. Related News [Odunaike kicks off Lagos governorship campaign, vows to fix state](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/odunaike-kicks-off-lagos-governorship-campaign-vows-to-fix-state/) [EPL: Man City beat Ipswich, Liverpool survive Brentford scare](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/epl-man-city-beat-ipswich-liverpool-survive-brentford-scare/) [Obi-Kwankwaso movement salutes music warriors fueling new Nigeria](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/obi-kwankwaso-movement-salutes-music-warriors-fueling-new-nigeria/) GameCenter Widget: businessday.ng [Iheanyi Nwachukwu](https://businessday.ng/author/iheanyi-nwachukwu/) Iheanyi Nwachukwu, is a creative content writer with over two decades journalism experience writing on banking, finance, capital markets, and tax. The multiple awards winning journalist is Deputy Editor, BusinessDay. Iheanyi holds BSc Degree in Economics from Imo State University; Master of Science (MSc) Degree in Management from University of Lagos. Iheanyi has attended several work-related trainings including (i) Advanced Writing and Reporting Skills (Pan African University, Lagos); (ii) News Agency Journalism (Indian Institute of Mass Communication {IIMC}, New Delhi, India); and (iii) Capital Markets Development and Regulations (International Law Institute {ILI} of Georgetown University, Washington DC, USA). Other trainings Iheanyi attended include: Economic/Political Risk Analysis (By Thomson Reuters Foundation); International Financial Journalism (IFJ) (By PMA Media Training, UK); Effective Business Writing Skills (By Phillips Consulting); Reporting on Corporate Governance (By International Finance Corporation (IFC) & Thomson Reuters Foundation UK); etc. In addition, he has participated in high-level economy & markets events in Dubai, South Africa, Morocco, and other African countries like Zambia, Ghana and Gambia. Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [Liquidity surge fuels speculative rally in low-cap stocks](https://businessday.ng/markets/article/liquidity-surge-fuels-speculative-rally-in-low-cap-stocks/) [NGX Group, partners advance cross-border investment collaboration through Dangote Refinery offer in Egypt, Ethiopia](https://businessday.ng/markets/article/ngx-group-partners-advance-cross-border-investment-collaboration-through-dangote-refinery-offer-in-egypt-ethiopia/) [Dangote Group, Botswana Exchange laud NGX Group for driving cross-border collaboration](https://businessday.ng/markets/article/dangote-group-botswana-exchange-laud-ngx-group-for-driving-cross-border-collaboration/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "12cf57b13be328ac42eaa521cad1b0b37f0d422793f2dc980e3f2674354ce090", "domain": "businessday", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:30:12Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1058", "title": "UNDC seeks PINL contract extension on reported oil production gains", "url": "https://businessday.ng/energy/oilandgas/article/undc-seeks-pinl-contract-extension-on-reported-oil-production-gains/", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/energy/oilandgas/article/undc-seeks-pinl-contract-extension-on-reported-oil-production-gains/", "text": "Share Advertisement The United Niger Delta Congress (UNDC) has urged the federal government to extend the pipeline surveillance contract of Pipeline Infrastructure Nigeria Limited (PINL), citing reported improvements in crude oil delivery, infrastructure protection and employment across the Niger Delta. The group said the company\u2019s reported performance, alongside investments in host communities, justified retaining the existing arrangement to sustain oil production gains and protect jobs in the region. The call followed a report by the technical subcommittee of the House of Representatives Committee on Host Communities, which investigated a petition against PINL and Osahon Okunbo, its managing director. According to the UNDC, the subcommittee found the allegations against the company and its leadership unsubstantiated and recommended that the petition be dismissed, further investigation discontinued and PINL\u2019s contract extended. In a note, the group said continuity in pipeline surveillance would help consolidate reported gains in crude oil production, protect petroleum infrastructure and strengthen confidence among businesses operating in Nigeria\u2019s oil and gas industry. Read also: [United Nigeria Airlines to raise N20bn through commercial paper](https://businessday.ng/aviation/article/united-nigeria-airlines-to-raise-n20bn-through-commercial-paper/) It highlighted an increase in crude oil deliveries through the Trans Niger Pipeline to the Bonny Terminal, from three percent when PINL commenced operations in August 2022 to 98 percent by September 2026. It also said Oil Mining Lease 11, which had been shut in for 18 months, resumed operations within 90 days of PINL\u2019s engagement. The group noted that the company had facilitated the remediation of 2,049 illegal pipeline connections and the destruction of 1,010 illegal refinery clusters, while conducting surveillance across 215 host communities. Beyond infrastructure protection, the UNDC said PINL had employed more than 35,600 surveillance guards drawn from Niger Delta communities, providing incomes for residents and creating alternatives to illegal oil-related activities. It said the company operated 28 forward bases, 130 boats, 175 vehicles and 25 drones to support surveillance across the region\u2019s waterways and creeks. Furthermore, UNDC noted more than 5,000 scholarships have been awarded to children from host communities and skills training provided to over 4,000 women and girls. It added that PINL had supported flood relief efforts in more than 10 communities in Rivers State. According to the note, these interventions demonstrate how pipeline surveillance arrangements can extend beyond security to support livelihoods, skills development and economic activity in oil-producing communities. Therefore, UNDC reiterated that extending PINL\u2019s contract would preserve the workforce and community relationships developed since the company\u2019s engagement, warning that an interruption could weaken existing pipeline protection structures in the Eastern corridor. It also said contract continuity could reinforce investor confidence by demonstrating that performance and operational results influence government contracting decisions. It urged the federal government to consider the subcommittee\u2019s reported recommendations and sustain measures aimed at improving crude oil production, protecting critical infrastructure and expanding economic opportunities in host communities. Related News [Gas pipeline communities give NNPC, PINL two weeks to pay surveillance arrears](https://businessday.ng/energy/oilandgas/article/gas-pipeline-communities-give-nnpc-pinl-two-weeks-to-pay-surveillance-arrears/) [Pipeline surveillance: Civil society group defends PINL, demands proof of \u20a62.7trn claim](https://businessday.ng/news/article/pipeline-surveillance-civil-society-group-defends-pinl-demands-proof-of-%e2%82%a62-7trn-claim/) [Axxela\u2019s subsidiary expands gas pipeline by 5.1km in Port Harcourt](https://businessday.ng/news/article/axxelas-subsidiary-expands-gas-pipeline-by-5-1km-in-port-harcourt/) GameCenter Widget: businessday.ng [Feyishola Jaiyesimi](https://businessday.ng/author/feyishola-jaiyesimi/) Feyishola Jaiyesimi is a writer and storyteller. She currently writes for the energy desk at BusinessDay with a focus on renewable energy and environmental issues. She began her journalism career as an agricultural reporter and now covers the energy sector, including oil, gas, electricity, environment, and renewables. She has been selected for professional training by the US Consulate, Lagos. She is a 2025 Dataphyte Biodiversity Reporting Fellow. Feyishola holds a bachelor\u2019s degree in Zoology and Environmental Biology from Ekiti State University. Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [NNPC isolates pipeline after Rivers explosion destroys properties](https://businessday.ng/energy/oilandgas/article/nnpc-isolates-pipeline-after-rivers-explosion-destroys-properties/) [Gas pipeline communities give NNPC, PINL two weeks to pay surveillance arrears](https://businessday.ng/energy/oilandgas/article/gas-pipeline-communities-give-nnpc-pinl-two-weeks-to-pay-surveillance-arrears/) [Inside Nigerian startup using solar power to rescue wasted crops](https://businessday.ng/energy/oilandgas/article/inside-nigerian-startup-using-solar-power-to-rescue-wasted-crops/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "c6f5814630d78939a90503ee6d348c1d0557b6f8c10ea6765dd974240be407c4", "domain": "businessday", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:30:13Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1059", "title": "NIMENA seeks national innovation hub as maritime, energy score hits 43.75%", "url": "https://businessday.ng/maritime/article/nimena-seeks-national-innovation-hub-as-maritime-energy-score-hits-43-75/", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/maritime/article/nimena-seeks-national-innovation-hub-as-maritime-energy-score-hits-43-75/", "text": "Share Advertisement The Nigerian Institution of Marine Engineers and Naval Architects (NIMENA) has urged the Federal Government and private sector to establish a unified national innovation agenda after an assessment scored Nigeria\u2019s maritime and energy innovation ecosystem 43.75 out of 100. The assessment, developed with global innovation platform Plug and Play, classified the ecosystem as \u201cEmerging\u201d, identifying skills gaps, weak industry collaboration and difficulties commercialising research as major constraints to indigenous technology development. The Maritime and Energy Ecosystem Capability Model (ECM) recommends a coordinated framework to connect government institutions, businesses, universities, investors and startups, to attract investment, develop skilled workers and expand local technology capacity. Speaking at the report\u2019s launch, Eferebo Sylvanus, NIMENA national chairman, said fragmented innovation initiatives were limiting the country\u2019s ability to harness opportunities in the maritime and energy sectors. \u201cWhile national startup acceleration programmes across the public and private sectors are laudable, working in isolation limits our collective impact,\u201d Sylvanus said. \u201cTo transform Nigeria\u2019s maritime and energy sectors into a global innovation hub, we must move past fragmentation and unite behind a coherent, structured framework.\u201d The assessment examined five dimensions: capital, network, society, initiatives and regulations, covering access to finance, institutional partnerships, talent development, innovation infrastructure and the regulatory environment. Regulations emerged as the ecosystem\u2019s strongest dimension, while network and society recorded the weakest performances, highlighting gaps in collaboration, specialised skills development and talent retention. The report identified three interconnected challenges: difficulty turning Nigeria\u2019s youthful population into industry-ready talent, a mismatch between corporate needs and local startups\u2019 technical capabilities, and inconsistent implementation of policies and funding mechanisms. It said startups struggling to secure commercial contracts often find it difficult to attract investment, while inadequate funding limits their ability to develop technologies that meet industry requirements. To address these constraints, NIMENA proposed a Maritime and Energy Innovation Hub to link local technology developers with corporate challenges, pilot projects, investors and international partners. The proposed hub would be developed through a phased five-year roadmap, with the goal of moving Nigeria\u2019s innovation ecosystem into the \u201cAdvanced\u201d category by 2030. Other recommendations include dedicated funding for maritime and energy startups, clearer regulatory pathways, supervised testing environments for emerging technologies and a Maritime Skills Activation Framework linking education and professional certification to industry needs. The report also identified remote condition monitoring, digital twins and virtual asset management as areas where stronger domestic capabilities could increase local participation in technology-driven industrial activities. Nigeria was benchmarked against Kenya, Egypt, South Africa and Singapore to examine approaches to investment mobilisation, institutional coordination, talent development and innovation support. Sylvanus said NIMENA would transmit the report to relevant government ministries, regulatory agencies, engineering bodies and the National Assembly as a policy advisory resource. The institution urged policymakers, industry leaders, academic institutions, investors and development partners to support implementation, arguing that coordinated action would be necessary to convert research into commercial technologies and create skilled jobs. Related News [Odunaike kicks off Lagos governorship campaign, vows to fix state](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/odunaike-kicks-off-lagos-governorship-campaign-vows-to-fix-state/) [EPL: Man City beat Ipswich, Liverpool survive Brentford scare](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/epl-man-city-beat-ipswich-liverpool-survive-brentford-scare/) [Obi-Kwankwaso movement salutes music warriors fueling new Nigeria](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/obi-kwankwaso-movement-salutes-music-warriors-fueling-new-nigeria/) GameCenter Widget: businessday.ng Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [UNDC seeks PINL contract extension on reported oil production gains](https://businessday.ng/energy/oilandgas/article/undc-seeks-pinl-contract-extension-on-reported-oil-production-gains/) [NNPC isolates pipeline after Rivers explosion destroys properties](https://businessday.ng/energy/oilandgas/article/nnpc-isolates-pipeline-after-rivers-explosion-destroys-properties/) [IEA speeds diesel releases as supply shortages persist](https://businessday.ng/energy/article/iea-speeds-diesel-releases-as-supply-shortages-persist/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "85bc825539d49694d93c5e566d1b075c11bcf1e3801d218fd15c08ac8bce1710", "domain": "businessday", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:30:14Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1060", "title": "NNPC isolates pipeline after Rivers explosion destroys properties", "url": "https://businessday.ng/energy/oilandgas/article/nnpc-isolates-pipeline-after-rivers-explosion-destroys-properties/", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/energy/oilandgas/article/nnpc-isolates-pipeline-after-rivers-explosion-destroys-properties/", "text": "Share Advertisement The Nigerian National Petroleum Company (NNPC) Limited has announced the activation of emergency containment protocols following a gas pipeline explosion in the Aleto community, Eleme Local Government Area of Rivers State. The blast, which occurred on Thursday on a pipeline operated by NNPC Gas Infrastructure Company Limited (NGIC), resulted in injuries to residents and damaged several commercial and residential properties in the area. To prevent further leakage or escalation, the company said it has shut off and isolated the affected pipeline segment. The state oil firm stated that it is collaborating with the Nigerian Midstream and Downstream Petroleum Regulatory Authority (NMDPRA), security agencies, and local emergency officials to coordinate relief and assess the environmental and structural damage. \u201cWe are deeply concerned by reports of injuries and damage to homes and businesses. Our thoughts are with those affected and their families. Protecting lives and preventing further harm are our immediate priorities. \u201cNGIC has activated its emergency response procedures and isolated the affected pipeline segment to limit further impact and is working with the Nigerian Midstream and Downstream Petroleum Regulatory Authority (NMDPRA), security agencies and other relevant stakeholders to coordinate the response and assess the impact on people, property and the environment. \u201cWe are also engaging the host community leadership to understand immediate needs and coordinate appropriate support for those affected,\u201d the company stated in statement issued to journalists. It also explained that the cause of the incident and the full extent of its impact are currently being assessed, adding relevant operational information, including previous reports and actions taken, will be reviewed to support a clear understanding of the circumstances surrounding the incident. \u201cWe will continue to work closely with the relevant authorities to establish the facts and determine appropriate corrective measures. \u201cResidents and members of the public are urged to keep clear of the affected area and follow safety instructions from emergency responders and the relevant authorities. NNPC Limited remains committed to the safety of its host communities, environmental protection and responsible operation of its facilities. We will provide further updates as verified information becomes available,\u201d it added. Related News [EPL: Man City beat Ipswich, Liverpool survive Brentford scare](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/epl-man-city-beat-ipswich-liverpool-survive-brentford-scare/) [Iwobi\u2019s goal seals Fulham\u2019s first Premier League win](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/iwobis-goal-seals-fulhams-first-premier-league-win/) [World Bank report: ADC asks Tinubu to explain missing $12.4bn](https://businessday.ng/news/article/world-bank-report-adc-asks-tinubu-to-explain-missing-12-4bn/) GameCenter Widget: businessday.ng Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [UNDC seeks PINL contract extension on reported oil production gains](https://businessday.ng/energy/oilandgas/article/undc-seeks-pinl-contract-extension-on-reported-oil-production-gains/) [Gas pipeline communities give NNPC, PINL two weeks to pay surveillance arrears](https://businessday.ng/energy/oilandgas/article/gas-pipeline-communities-give-nnpc-pinl-two-weeks-to-pay-surveillance-arrears/) [Inside Nigerian startup using solar power to rescue wasted crops](https://businessday.ng/energy/oilandgas/article/inside-nigerian-startup-using-solar-power-to-rescue-wasted-crops/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "5a6d5b2078c5c900e85f4bb97c786d51c624b6fc7f6b6712f5b5ae428f6f3487", "domain": "businessday", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:30:16Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1061", "title": "IEA speeds diesel releases as supply shortages persist", "url": "https://businessday.ng/energy/article/iea-speeds-diesel-releases-as-supply-shortages-persist/", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/energy/article/iea-speeds-diesel-releases-as-supply-shortages-persist/", "text": "Share Advertisement The International Energy Agency (IEA) will release more than 20 million barrels of diesel as soon as possible and accelerate the remaining deliveries under its emergency oil stock draw, as supply disruptions continue to tighten global fuel markets. The move will push total releases beyond the 400 million barrels pledged in March, following the crisis around the Strait of Hormuz. About 330 million barrels have already been released, while roughly 100 million barrels remain to be delivered under the coordinated action. Some member countries have exceeded their initial commitments, while others have yet to release their full allocations. However, the immediate diesel release fell short of market expectations. IEA members had discussed releasing as much as 50 million barrels of diesel as part of a broader 100-million-barrel package of crude oil and refined products. European gasoil prices rose after Thursday\u2019s announcement, suggesting traders had anticipated a larger immediate supply boost. The United States has also pressed other IEA members to make additional emergency supplies available as diesel prices remain near record levels ahead of the November 3 midterm elections. Spain plans to release another 4.4 million barrels from its strategic reserves in October. It has delivered 3.7 million barrels of the 11.5 million barrels it pledged in March. Diesel remains a pressure point in the global fuel market, with refinery disruptions in the Middle East and reduced Russian fuel production curbing exports even as crude shipments through the Persian Gulf begin to recover. The accelerated releases aim to bring more supply to the market sooner. Still, the scale of the immediate diesel allocation has disappointed traders seeking greater relief from tight refined-product supplies. Related News [Odunaike kicks off Lagos governorship campaign, vows to fix state](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/odunaike-kicks-off-lagos-governorship-campaign-vows-to-fix-state/) [EPL: Man City beat Ipswich, Liverpool survive Brentford scare](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/epl-man-city-beat-ipswich-liverpool-survive-brentford-scare/) [Obi-Kwankwaso movement salutes music warriors fueling new Nigeria](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/obi-kwankwaso-movement-salutes-music-warriors-fueling-new-nigeria/) GameCenter Widget: businessday.ng Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [UNDC seeks PINL contract extension on reported oil production gains](https://businessday.ng/energy/oilandgas/article/undc-seeks-pinl-contract-extension-on-reported-oil-production-gains/) [NIMENA seeks national innovation hub as maritime, energy score hits 43.75%](https://businessday.ng/maritime/article/nimena-seeks-national-innovation-hub-as-maritime-energy-score-hits-43-75/) [NNPC isolates pipeline after Rivers explosion destroys properties](https://businessday.ng/energy/oilandgas/article/nnpc-isolates-pipeline-after-rivers-explosion-destroys-properties/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "0e9dab4a8f2d2b116bff3701efd24818ad20040a839a175e076d39be7da6a1a6", "domain": "businessday", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:30:17Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1062", "title": "Nigeria\u2019s LPG marketers seek tighter safety rules, digital controls", "url": "https://businessday.ng/energy/article/nigerias-lpg-marketers-seek-tighter-safety-rules-digital-controls/", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/energy/article/nigerias-lpg-marketers-seek-tighter-safety-rules-digital-controls/", "text": "Share Advertisement Nigeria\u2019s liquefied petroleum gas marketers are pressing operators to put safety, regulatory compliance and digital tools at the centre of the industry\u2019s growth as the country works to expand cooking-gas use. The Nigerian Association of Liquefied Petroleum Gas Marketers, or NALPGAM, made the call after its 2026 webinar, \u201cDownstream LPG Operational Excellence and Compliance.\u201d The event brought together operators, regulators and other stakeholders to discuss safety risks, market volatility, operational losses and regulatory requirements. \u201cThe future of Nigeria\u2019s LPG industry cannot be achieved by increasing volumes alone,\u201d Edu Inyang, the association\u2019s national president, said in his opening address. \u201cWe must grow safely, responsibly, efficiently and sustainably. Safety is not an occasional campaign; it must become a continuous industry culture.\u201d A key outcome was the adoption of an aspiration for a \u201cNALPGAM Standard of Operational Excellence.\u201d It rests on four principles: safe and well-maintained facilities, competent and trained operators, compliant processes, and customer protection. Participants urged operators to build safety into daily operations and strengthen preventive maintenance. They also called for compliance with licensing and incident-reporting requirements and for an end to unsafe practices, including unauthorised propane blending and illegal decanting. Inyang framed compliance as a commercial matter as well as a legal one. \u201cCompliance is not merely a regulatory burden; it is a business advantage,\u201d he said. \u201cWell-managed LPG operations reduce accidents, improve efficiency, protect investments and strengthen public confidence in our industry.\u201d The webinar also pointed to digital technologies as practical ways to cut product losses, improve accountability and raise efficiency. These included smart filling systems, automated tank gauges, load cells, flow meters and vehicle tracking. The discussion comes as Nigeria targets LPG consumption of about 5 million metric tons a year by 2030. Stakeholders called for greater industry participation in the Decade of Gas LPG penetration program. They also urged stronger collaboration to widen access, improve cylinder traceability and promote certified local manufacturing. NALPGAM asked regulators and program coordinators to involve its members in carrying out LPG penetration and cylinder recirculation initiatives. The association said its members\u2019 nationwide reach and closeness to consumers make them well placed to help. \u201cOur aspiration is clear: every LPG facility must be safe and properly maintained, every operator competent and adequately trained, every process compliant, and every customer protected,\u201d Inyang said. \u201cThis is the standard of operational excellence we must collectively pursue.\u201d The association reaffirmed its commitment to working with government, regulators and industry stakeholders to build a safer, more efficient and competitive LPG market. Related News [Odunaike kicks off Lagos governorship campaign, vows to fix state](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/odunaike-kicks-off-lagos-governorship-campaign-vows-to-fix-state/) [EPL: Man City beat Ipswich, Liverpool survive Brentford scare](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/epl-man-city-beat-ipswich-liverpool-survive-brentford-scare/) [Obi-Kwankwaso movement salutes music warriors fueling new Nigeria](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/obi-kwankwaso-movement-salutes-music-warriors-fueling-new-nigeria/) GameCenter Widget: businessday.ng [Oladehinde Oladipo](https://businessday.ng/author/oladehinde-oladipobusinessdayonline-com/) Dipo Oladehinde is a skilled energy analyst with experience across Nigeria's energy sector alongside relevant know-how about Nigeria\u2019s macro economy. He provides a blend of market intelligence, financial analysis, industry insight, micro and macro-level analysis of a wide range of local and international issues as well as informed technical rudiments for policy-making and private directions. Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [UNDC seeks PINL contract extension on reported oil production gains](https://businessday.ng/energy/oilandgas/article/undc-seeks-pinl-contract-extension-on-reported-oil-production-gains/) [NIMENA seeks national innovation hub as maritime, energy score hits 43.75%](https://businessday.ng/maritime/article/nimena-seeks-national-innovation-hub-as-maritime-energy-score-hits-43-75/) [NNPC isolates pipeline after Rivers explosion destroys properties](https://businessday.ng/energy/oilandgas/article/nnpc-isolates-pipeline-after-rivers-explosion-destroys-properties/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "ece3d4452c2dad2b5e37b38b08206c809b795ec49883709e9fba3255e41e41b8", "domain": "businessday", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:30:18Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1063", "title": "Odunaike kicks off Lagos governorship campaign, vows to fix state", "url": "https://businessday.ng/politics/article/odunaike-kicks-off-lagos-governorship-campaign-vows-to-fix-state/", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/politics/article/odunaike-kicks-off-lagos-governorship-campaign-vows-to-fix-state/", "text": "Share Advertisement \u2026Unveils 162-page manifesto, promises inclusive governance Rotimi Odunaike, Lagos State governorship candidate of the Labour Party (LP), on Friday formally launched his campaign with a promise to fix the state\u2019s infrastructure deficit, expand access to social amenities and end what he described as decades of governance that favoured a privileged few. Odunaike, who unveiled a 162-page campaign manifesto titled \u2018Our Covenant With the People of Lagos\u2019, said his administration would place residents at the centre of policymaking and pursue an inclusive development agenda. Speaking at the campaign flag-off in Ikeja, he argued that Lagos had largely served the interests of a privileged few since the return to democratic rule in 1999, leaving many residents grappling with deteriorating infrastructure and inadequate social amenities. \u201cI will fix Lagos. Our slogan is \u2018A New Lagos for All\u2019. This is not just a mere slogan, but a determination to put the people at the centre of my administration,\u201d he said. \u201cMy deputy, Prince Lateef Adenkule Adele, and I will fix Lagos and make it work for all.\u201d The candidate pledged to begin addressing the state\u2019s challenges from the day he is sworn into office, although the specific policy proposals and implementation timelines contained in his manifesto were not detailed in the statement announcing the campaign launch. The event was attended by the Labour Party\u2019s National Chairman, Senator Nenadi Usman, and members of the party\u2019s National Working Committee. Read also: [Obi-Kwankwaso movement salutes music warriors fueling new Nigeria](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/obi-kwankwaso-movement-salutes-music-warriors-fueling-new-nigeria/) Usman urged party leaders and members in Lagos to put internal differences aside and mobilise support for Odunaike, saying unity would be critical to the party\u2019s electoral prospects. \u201cI charge you to surpass the feat the Labour Party recorded in 2023. I am certain that with unity of purpose, you can achieve this because the Labour Party is a party of the grassroots in Lagos State,\u201d she said. Her remarks underscored the party\u2019s push to consolidate its grassroots base and build on its performance in the 2023 general election. Odunaike\u2019s emergence as the party\u2019s governorship standard-bearer comes with an emphasis on professional experience and administrative capacity. The Labour Party described him as a banking and finance expert and entrepreneur with interests in agriculture, technology and artificial intelligence. He previously served as chairman of the Labour Party in Lagos State, a position in which the party said he helped reconcile rival factions and restore cohesion during a period of internal crisis. The campaign launch sets the stage for the party\u2019s effort to challenge the existing political order in Lagos, Nigeria\u2019s commercial hub, with Odunaike positioning his candidacy around inclusive governance, infrastructure renewal and improved access to public services. His campaign will now have to translate its promises into a clear, costed and measurable policy programme capable of convincing voters that a change in leadership can deliver tangible improvements to their daily lives. Related News [EPL: Man City beat Ipswich, Liverpool survive Brentford scare](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/epl-man-city-beat-ipswich-liverpool-survive-brentford-scare/) [Obi-Kwankwaso movement salutes music warriors fueling new Nigeria](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/obi-kwankwaso-movement-salutes-music-warriors-fueling-new-nigeria/) [2027: Ilaji Resort boss calls on supporters to verify PVCs, dedicates birthday to Tinubu](https://businessday.ng/news/article/2027-ilaji-resort-boss-calls-on-supporters-to-verify-pvcs-dedicates-birthday-to-tinubu/) GameCenter Widget: businessday.ng Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [Why Africa must convert diplomatic access into industrial assets](https://businessday.ng/life/article/why-africa-must-convert-diplomatic-access-into-industrial-assets/) [Presidential vacation: Nigeria\u2019s legacy of leadership vacuum or the new normal?](https://businessday.ng/life/article/presidential-vacation-nigerias-legacy-of-leadership-vacuum-or-the-new-normal/) [Abia seeks increased private-sector investment in clean cooking solutions](https://businessday.ng/life/article/abia-seeks-increased-private-sector-investment-in-clean-cooking-solutions/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "4177884cb7c46653b2bdaef893d2bcbacd4ed2e96a2a30998fcd3d4c8002b958", "domain": "businessday", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:30:19Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1064", "title": "Obi-Kwankwaso movement salutes music warriors fueling new Nigeria", "url": "https://businessday.ng/politics/article/obi-kwankwaso-movement-salutes-music-warriors-fueling-new-nigeria/", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/politics/article/obi-kwankwaso-movement-salutes-music-warriors-fueling-new-nigeria/", "text": "Share Advertisement The Obi-Kwankwaso Movement has praised Nigerian musicians both at home and in the diaspora for turning music into a tool of hope ahead of the 2027 elections. In a statement issued on Saturday by its media office, the Peter Obi Media Reach (POMR) stated that music has become the heartbeat of national renewal, amplifying the political ambitions of millions. POMR highlighted three breakout voices driving the movement: KASI, an Anambra-born sensation whose anthem Peter Obi or Nothing has emerged as a rallying cry; Tijjani Gandu, whose Hausa hit Obi-Kwankwaso Clear is resonating across the North; and Mr DapBoy, whose soulful track Lokaci expresses unwavering support for Peter Obi\u2019s 2027 presidential ambition. Cultural Heritage and National Discourse Idris Zekeri Jnr, the POMR spokesman, noted that these artists are shaping national discourse and equipping young Nigerians with a bold language to demand systemic transformation. \u201cYour talent is valuable, your creativity merits recognition, and your voices form an essential part of the nation\u2019s cultural heritage,\u201d Idris Zekeri Jnr said in the statement. Celebrating Nigeria\u2019s rich cultural diversity, spanning Igbo highlife, Hausa melodies, Yoruba rhythms, and Pidgin, POMR encouraged artists to keep using their craft to foster peace, unity, and constructive dialogue. World Singing Day is observed annually on the third Saturday of October. Related News [Odunaike kicks off Lagos governorship campaign, vows to fix state](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/odunaike-kicks-off-lagos-governorship-campaign-vows-to-fix-state/) [EPL: Man City beat Ipswich, Liverpool survive Brentford scare](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/epl-man-city-beat-ipswich-liverpool-survive-brentford-scare/) [2027: Ilaji Resort boss calls on supporters to verify PVCs, dedicates birthday to Tinubu](https://businessday.ng/news/article/2027-ilaji-resort-boss-calls-on-supporters-to-verify-pvcs-dedicates-birthday-to-tinubu/) GameCenter Widget: businessday.ng Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [Patrick Dele Cole: The diplomat who stood up to power and helped reshape Nigerian journalism](https://businessday.ng/life-arts/article/patrick-dele-cole-the-diplomat-who-stood-up-to-power-and-helped-reshape-nigerian-journalism/) [EWA launches grant pool to fund African creative businesses](https://businessday.ng/life-arts/article/ewa-launches-grant-pool-to-fund-african-creative-businesses/) [Ikogosi\u2013Ilara Mokin: Emerging hospitality cluster, takes off](https://businessday.ng/life-arts/article/ikogosi-ilara-mokin-emerging-hospitality-cluster-takes-off/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "3f0b40d85dbb5b5064222baa54f617bdb1dbe47d07a2cd2e2286e5294a1a3242", "domain": "businessday", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:30:21Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1065", "title": "Tinubu nominates six new INEC commissioners ahead of 2027 elections", "url": "https://businessday.ng/politics/article/tinubu-nominates-six-new-inec-commissioners-ahead-of-2027-elections/", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/politics/article/tinubu-nominates-six-new-inec-commissioners-ahead-of-2027-elections/", "text": "Share Advertisement President Bola Tinubu on Friday, nominated four new national commissioners and two new resident electoral commissioners to fill vacancies at the Independent National Electoral Commission created by expiring tenures. Bayo Onanuga, Special Adviser to the President on Information and Strategy, said the names of the nominees have been forwarded to the Senate for scrutiny and confirmation Onanuga said the President has also reappointed Bala Bila, the national commissioner representing the North East. The new national commissioners are Mustapha Aliu, who will replace Abdullahi Zuru as the North West representative; and Bashir Muhammed Umar, who will replace Muhammad Adamu as the North Central representative. Tinubu also recommended Oluwatoyin Babalola, the resident electoral commissioner in Osun, as the new South West national commissioner to replace Cornelius Ajayi, whose tenure expires on October 24. The two new resident electoral commissioners are retired permanent secretary Samuel Akinleye, to represent Ekiti State, and Zahradden Basiru Sanusi, from Jigawa, who will replace Saidu Babura. Tinubu therefore, urged the Senate to expedite the nominees\u2019 confirmation in view of the forthcoming general elections next January. Related News [EPL: Man City beat Ipswich, Liverpool survive Brentford scare](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/epl-man-city-beat-ipswich-liverpool-survive-brentford-scare/) [Iwobi\u2019s goal seals Fulham\u2019s first Premier League win](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/iwobis-goal-seals-fulhams-first-premier-league-win/) [World Bank report: ADC asks Tinubu to explain missing $12.4bn](https://businessday.ng/news/article/world-bank-report-adc-asks-tinubu-to-explain-missing-12-4bn/) GameCenter Widget: businessday.ng Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [Odunaike kicks off Lagos governorship campaign, vows to fix state](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/odunaike-kicks-off-lagos-governorship-campaign-vows-to-fix-state/) [Obi-Kwankwaso movement salutes music warriors fueling new Nigeria](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/obi-kwankwaso-movement-salutes-music-warriors-fueling-new-nigeria/) [2027: APC plans nationwide rallies, to kick off in Kaduna](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/2027-apc-plans-nationwide-rallies-to-kick-off-in-kaduna/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "699e7c29b7d26870a00eec981019a9c596870634bb1844b6f4def2d52d50a278", "domain": "businessday", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:30:22Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1066", "title": "2027: APC plans nationwide rallies, to kick off in Kaduna", "url": "https://businessday.ng/politics/article/2027-apc-plans-nationwide-rallies-to-kick-off-in-kaduna/", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/politics/article/2027-apc-plans-nationwide-rallies-to-kick-off-in-kaduna/", "text": "Share Advertisement \u2026Tinubu to attend in Lagos, Kano, Rivers, 9 other states The All Progressives Congress (APC) has unveiled a proposed nationwide campaign rally schedule ahead of the 2027 general elections, with activities expected to begin in Kaduna State on October 24 and culminate in a grand rally in Lagos on January 9, 2027. The schedule, released by the ruling party outlines campaign stops across the six geopolitical zones, with 12 locations designated for the expected attendance of President Bola Tinubu. The proposed rallies will test the APC\u2019s nationwide mobilisation capacity as it prepares to defend its control of the presidency and consolidate its electoral strength ahead of the January 16, 2027 presidential election. According to the schedule, Kaduna will host the opening rally on October 24, followed by Calabar, Cross River State, on November 2, and Gombe, Gombe State, on November 3. Other November destinations include Lafia, Nasarawa State; Ado-Ekiti, Ekiti State; Port Harcourt, Rivers State; Abakaliki, Ebonyi State; Dutse, Jigawa State; Akure, Ondo State; Benin City, Edo State; Jos, Plateau State; Yenagoa, Bayelsa State; Damaturu, Yobe State; Birnin Kebbi, Kebbi State; Uyo, Akwa Ibom State; Ibadan, Oyo State; Maiduguri, Borno State; Enugu, Enugu State; Bauchi, Bauchi State and Awka, Anambra State. The December leg will cover Makurdi, Benue State; Katsina, Katsina State; Abeokuta, Ogun State; Sokoto, Sokoto State; Umuahia, Abia State; Lokoja, Kogi State; Osogbo, Osun State; Ilorin, Kwara State; Asaba, Delta State; Gusau, Zamfara State; Minna, Niger State; Kano, Kano State; Jalingo, Taraba State; Abuja, the Federal Capital Territory; and Owerri, Imo State. The campaign will continue in January with a rally in Yola, Adamawa State, on January 4, before the proposed grand finale in Lagos on January 9. The 12 locations marked for Tinubu\u2019s expected attendance are Kaduna, Port Harcourt, Jos, Ibadan, Maiduguri, Enugu, Bauchi, Sokoto, Asaba, Kano, Yola and Lagos. However, the APC cautioned that the programme was not final, stating that \u201cthis is a proposed schedule and is subject to official confirmation.\u201d The party added that the timetable remained subject to confirmation and possible adjustments. Tinubu had inaugurated the APC Presidential Campaign Council in Abuja, where he said he would not attend every campaign rally, citing the need to balance electioneering with governance, economic responsibilities and efforts to attract investors. The President urged campaign council members to take the lead in mobilising support for the party across the country. GameCenter Widget: businessday.ng Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [Odunaike kicks off Lagos governorship campaign, vows to fix state](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/odunaike-kicks-off-lagos-governorship-campaign-vows-to-fix-state/) [Obi-Kwankwaso movement salutes music warriors fueling new Nigeria](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/obi-kwankwaso-movement-salutes-music-warriors-fueling-new-nigeria/) [Tinubu nominates six new INEC commissioners ahead of 2027 elections](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/tinubu-nominates-six-new-inec-commissioners-ahead-of-2027-elections/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "2ccd505299b0f8a76c32c363dc79eec3a67e3ec6f81c97e6620a027747e4c950", "domain": "businessday", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:30:23Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1067", "title": "Court adjourns El-Rufai\u2019s N8.68bn contract fraud trial", "url": "https://businessday.ng/politics/article/court-adjourns-el-rufais-n8-68bn-contract-fraud-trial/", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/politics/article/court-adjourns-el-rufais-n8-68bn-contract-fraud-trial/", "text": "Share Advertisement The Federal High Court in Kaduna has adjourned the trial of Nasir El-Rufai, former Governor of Kaduna State Governor, and six other defendants over alleged corruption and money laundering involving an N8.68 billion closed-circuit television (CCTV) surveillance contract to December 8, 2026. The adjournment followed changes in the legal teams representing the Independent Corrupt Practices and Other Related Offences Commission (ICPC), which is prosecuting the case, and El-Rufai, the first defendant. The matter came up on Thursday before Hauwa\u2019u Buhari, Justice of the Federal High Court, Kaduna Judicial Division, where Ibrahim Muktar, the newly appointed prosecution counsel, announced his appearance and informed the court of changes in the prosecution team. Muktar subsequently requested an adjournment to enable him to study the case file, review previous proceedings and familiarise himself with the issues involved in the case to ensure effective prosecution. Similarly, Mustapha Abubakar, a Senior Advocate of Nigeria (SAN), who recently took over El-Rufai\u2019s defence, sought time to acquaint himself with the details of the case. The court granted the requests after considering the submissions of both counsel and fixed December 8 for the commencement of the substantive hearing. Meanwhile, the court adjourned until Friday, October 16, 2026, for ruling on a pending bail application filed by the second defendant, Jimi Adebisi Lawal, a former Senior Special Adviser and Counsellor to El-Rufai. The ICPC disclosed these developments in a statement issued on Thursday by Okor Odey, its Head of Media and Public Communications. The case arose from an amended 11-count charge filed by the anti-corruption agency against El-Rufai, Lawal and five corporate entities over the award and execution of a CCTV surveillance contract valued at approximately N8.68 billion during El-Rufai\u2019s administration as governor of Kaduna Stat. The former governor and the other defendants were arraigned on June 25, 2026, before the same court. The charge, marked FHC/KD/93C/2026, lists El-Rufai as the first defendant and Lawal as the second defendant. The five corporate defendants are Singularity Network Security Limited, Solar Life Nigeria Limited, Knowledge Investment Nigeria Limited, Intercellular Nigeria Limited and Noble Coast Resources Limited. According to the ICPC, the allegations include the award of the surveillance contract in violation of applicable public procurement requirements and the involvement of a company allegedly lacking the requisite experience and qualifications to execute the project. The defendants have denied the allegations. The ICPC said it remained committed to prosecuting the case in accordance with due process, the rule of law and the constitutional rights of all parties involved. Related News [EPL: Man City beat Ipswich, Liverpool survive Brentford scare](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/epl-man-city-beat-ipswich-liverpool-survive-brentford-scare/) [Iwobi\u2019s goal seals Fulham\u2019s first Premier League win](https://businessday.ng/sports/article/iwobis-goal-seals-fulhams-first-premier-league-win/) [World Bank report: ADC asks Tinubu to explain missing $12.4bn](https://businessday.ng/news/article/world-bank-report-adc-asks-tinubu-to-explain-missing-12-4bn/) GameCenter Widget: businessday.ng Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [Odunaike kicks off Lagos governorship campaign, vows to fix state](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/odunaike-kicks-off-lagos-governorship-campaign-vows-to-fix-state/) [Obi-Kwankwaso movement salutes music warriors fueling new Nigeria](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/obi-kwankwaso-movement-salutes-music-warriors-fueling-new-nigeria/) [Tinubu nominates six new INEC commissioners ahead of 2027 elections](https://businessday.ng/politics/article/tinubu-nominates-six-new-inec-commissioners-ahead-of-2027-elections/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "f60bfdacc61ae75c2d2e378d0b05fae014cb3dfb9d03fb522c3399f63a762cb2", "domain": "businessday", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:30:25Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1068", "title": "Nigeria\u2019s 2027 candidates must show how they will deliver", "url": "https://businessday.ng/pro/article/nigerias-2027-candidates-must-show-how-they-will-deliver/", "final_url": "https://businessday.ng/pro/article/nigerias-2027-candidates-must-show-how-they-will-deliver/", "text": "Share Advertisement Nigeria is approaching another general election amid persistent economic hardship, insecurity, weak infrastructure and declining public confidence in governance. Although the economy grew by 4.43Subscribe to keep reading GameCenter Widget: businessday.ng Advertisement Share You Might Also Like [17 firms pay shareholders N1.13trn in dividends in H1](https://businessday.ng/market-intelligence/article/17-firms-pay-shareholders-n1-13trn-in-dividends-in-h1/) [World Bank\u2019s October 2026 assessment and Fitch\u2019s upgrade to a positive outlook: Has Nigeria turned the corner?](https://businessday.ng/backpage/article/world-banks-october-2026-assessment-and-fitchs-upgrade-to-a-positive-outlook-has-nigeria-turned-the-corner/) [\u201cLogistics\u201d has become a convenient word for theft in Nigeria](https://businessday.ng/pro/article/logistics-has-become-a-convenient-word-for-theft-in-nigeria/) Advertisement", "content_hash": "50bef5284c957dc49e86d4672c2781edebb3e4997b76b7d7a04cdaf99e6832e6", "domain": "businessday", "fetched_at": "2026-10-18T04:30:28Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1069", "title": "NDIC \u2014 Nigeria Deposit Insurance Corporation", "url": "https://ndic.gov.ng/", "final_url": "https://ndic.gov.ng/", "text": "How we insure\nyour deposits\nNDIC coverage requires no registration and no fees. Deposits held in any CBN-licensed bank are automatically insured.\nStep 01\nPremium Assessment &amp; D\neposit Insurance Fund\nEvery deposit-taking financial institution licensed by the Central Bank of Nigeria (CBN) \u2014 commercial banks, MFBs, PMIs and non-interest banks \u2014 is covered by the NDIC and mandated to pay annual premiums based on its deposit liabilities and risk profile. These premiums are pooled into the Deposit Insurance Fund (DIF).\nStep 02\nSupervision &amp; Risk Monitoring\nNDIC collaborates with the CBN to supervise banks to reduce the potential risk of failure, and ensures that unsafe and unsound banking practices do not go unchecked.\nStep 03\nBank Licence Revocation\nIf the CBN revokes the licence of a bank, the NDIC is appointed as the liquidator and uses the DIF to pay depositors up to the insured limit.\nStep 04\nClaims &amp; Reimbursement\nUsing your Bank Verification Number (BVN), NDIC locates your other bank accounts and automatically pays your insured sum.\nStep 05\nLiquidation\nFor depositors whose balances exceed the insured limit, NDIC begins the recovery of the failed bank\u2019s assets, debts and investments. These funds are used to pay depositors\u2019 balances as liquidation dividends on a pro-rat\na basis.\nNews &amp; Publications\nNDIC Updates\nNDIC Hosts IADI Africa Regional Committee Annual Meetingg and Workshop\nJuly 2026\n2nd Quarter Citizens&#039; and Stakeholders&#039; Engagement Session held at FMF Abuja.\nJune 2026\nNDIC Management hosted the Chief Registrar of the Federal High Court, Mr. Yahaya Yakubu Shafa.\nJune 2026\nBanking System Stability: NDIC, NCGC to promote Unified Risk Management, Early Warning System\nMay 2026\nMore News\nHon. Minister of Finance&#039;s Keynote Speech at IADI-ARC Workshop 2026\nJuly 22, 2026\nMD/CE NDIC\u2019S Welcome Remark at IADI-ARC Workshop &amp; Annual Meeting 2026\nNDIC Hosts IADI Africa Regional Committee Meeting 2026\nJuly 17, 2026\nUpdate on the Revocation of the Operating\nLicenses of 46 Micro-Fina\nnce Banks by the Central Bank of Nigeria\nJul 1, 2026\nThe NDIC Story in Motion", "content_hash": "3f53592f6ae2aa6adc0fec63a455b3135f792abcdf38d8281a0ae20fe19211c9", "domain": "ndic.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-08T17:13:49.648Z", "language": "en"}, {"id": "1070", "title": "Welcome to SEC Nigeria\n                \n            \n            \n                \n                - Securities and Exchange Commission, Nigeria", "url": "https://sec.gov.ng/", "final_url": "https://sec.gov.ng/", "text": "For Investors\nFind a Registered Operator\nKeep Track of Circulars\nWhistleblowing\nKnown Investment Scams\nFor Operators\nOperator Registration\nKeep Track of Capital Market Data\nStatistical Bulletin\nNet Asset Value Data\nQuarterly PE &amp; Infra. Funds\nExecuted Transactions\nMarket Review and Outlook\nMergers, Acquisitions &amp; Takeovers\nFor Businesses\nRegister a Company\nBecome an Operator\nRaising Capital\nProcurements and Tenders\nOur Mandate\nRegulation\nActs\nRules and Regulations\nCodes of Conduct\nGuidelines\nPolicies\nDevelopment\nInvestor Education\nInsights for Operators\nInsights for Businesses\nThe Capital Market Master Plan\nEnforcements\nCos. Facing Enforcement Action\nReferred Cases\nA.P.C. Matters\nLitigatio