tion Dashboard Q4-2025 Pension Industry Information Dashboard Q3-2025 Pension Industry Information Dashboard Q1-2025 Pension Industry Information Dashboard Q4-2024 Pension Industry Information Dashboard Q3-2024 Pension Industry Information Dashboard Q1-2026 First Quarter Report 2026 Circular on RLA Custodial Fees Structure and Processes Monthly Report \u2013 June 2026 Circular Granting Forbearance to PFAs to Invest in Shares and other Securities of PFCs Circular on I am Alive Verification Exercise Circular on Digitization Guidelines on Stewardship Responsibilities by PFA Monthly Report \u2013 May 2026 Fourth Quarter Report 2025 Monthly Report \u2013 April 2026 Circular on Dangote Refinery Dr. Oyindasola Oluremi Oni Mr. Anyim Chinenye Nyerere Circular \u2013 Approval of Advertisement and Media Campaign Materials by Licensed Pension Fund Administrators Monthly Report \u2013 March 2026 Monthly Report \u2013 February 2026 Uncredited Contribution Employers List 2026 Addendum to the Revised Regulations on Investment of Pension Fund Assets Circular for Fund V, VII and APA fees ISMS Policy Document Monthly Report \u2013 January 2026 Guidelines for Verification and Enrolment Exercise of Prospective Retirees CIRCULAR \u2013 Categorisation of Contributions under the PPP Circular on Revised Documentation for Data Recapture Framework for the Engagement of Accredited Pension Agents in the Implementation of PPP Guidelines on PPP Monthly Report \u2013 December 2025 Monthly Report \u2013 November 2025 Circular on Appointment of External Auditors and Actuaries for Approved Existing Schemes and Additional Benefits Schemes Circular on Pension Increases for Federal Government Self-funded Agencies Third Quarter Report 2025 Monthly Report \u2013 October 2025 Second Quarter Report 2025 PenCom Approves two additional new Payment Solution Service Providers (PSSPs) Pension Contribution Remittance System (PCRS) Monthly Report \u2013 September 2025 ADDENDUM \u2013 Circular on Minimum Information to be Displayed on PFA\u2019s Website PenCom & NAICOM Joint Circular on Additional Inflow Circular on Retiree Life Annuity (RLA) Pension Clearance Certificate (PCC) Checklist for Approved Pension Agents (APAs) License Application APA License Application Form Advert For Expression of Interest Monthly Report August 2025 Monthly Report July 2025 Circular on Counterparties to Pension Funds Transactions in Government Securities Revised Regulatory Capital Requirement for Pension Fund Operators Circular on Compensation Framework for Approved Pension Agents (APAs) Framework for the Engagement of Accredited Pension Agents (APA) in the Implementation of Personal Pension Plan (PPP) Guidelines on Personal Pension Plan (PPP) The Revised Regulation on Investment of Pension Fund Assets Circular Securities Lending and Repurchase Transactions (Repos) By Pension Fund Administrators Revised Guideline on Corporate Governance for Licensed Pension Fund Operators Revised Guidelines on Appointments to Board and Senior Management Guidelines On Significant Cross-Shareholding In Licensed Pension Fund Operators Circular on Shared Services Arrangements with Related Parties of Licensed Pension Fund Operators CIRCULAR ON SHARED SERVICES ARRANGEMENTS WITH RELATED PARTIES OF LPFOs Guidelines on Foreign Currency Pension Contributions Addendum to Circular on Inducement of RSA Holders Investment in Additional Tier 1 (AT1) Capital Of Deposit Money Banks President Tinubu orders Free Healthcare for Low-Income Retirees and Pension Increases President Bola Tinubu has directed the expedited rollout of the free healthcare access initiative for low-income retirees under the Contributory Pension Scheme (CPS), describing it as a critical component of social protection and dignity in retirement. He also called for the prompt implementation of long-overdue pension increases and a minimum pension guarantee, which would provide a safety net for the most vulnerable pensioners under the CPS. The President gave the directives after receiving a briefing from the Director General (DG). The President further mandated the DG to urgently resolve the longstanding police pension issue, emphasizing that members of the police force who serve and protect the nation deserve to retire with dignity and peace of mind. The DG also briefed the President on ongoing efforts to ensure the value preservation of pension fund assets, especially in the face of inflation and macroeconomic pressures, and plans to introduce foreign currency contributions to enable Nigerians in the diaspora to participate in the pension system. The President strongly supported the reforms, reaffirming his administration\u2019s commitment to inclusive growth and protection for ordinary Nigerians. During the briefing, the DG updated the President on a suite of transformative pension initiatives to enhance retiree welfare and expand the Pension Scheme\u2019s reach. Addendum to Circular on Shareholding First Quarter Report, 2025 REVISED ICT GUIDELINES FOR PENSION FUND OPERATORS June 2025 Monthly Summary Parthian Pensions Limited 1st Floor, NIJ House, Plot 20, Adeyemo Alakija Street, Victoria Island, Lagos PenCom and the Head of Service are working together to introduce a Gratuity Scheme for federal civil servants under the Contributory Pension Scheme (CPS). For the first time, treasury-funded MDAs may soon enjoy gratuity in addition to pensions! This development followed a high-level meeting held on June 13, 2025, in Abuja, where the Director General of PenCom, Ms. Omolola Oloworaran, paid a courtesy visit to the Head of the Civil Service of the Federation (HCSF), Mrs. Didi Esther Walson-Jack. What\u2019s Changing? The proposed Gratuity Scheme will offer civil servants 100% of their last gross annual pay as a gratuity upon retirement. This is a major push to reward those who\u2019ve served Nigeria diligently. More Good News from PenCom! From August 2025, PenCom will launch a comprehensive online enrolment exercise to: Verify accrued pension rights; Establish liabilities and Enable government to possibly clear all pension arrears at once! PenCom 2nd Quarter 2025 Press Briefing PenCom Announces Landmark Reforms to Strengthen Nigeria\u2019s Pension System at the 2nd Quarter Press Briefing held in Lagos recently. The PenCom DG, Omolola Oloworaran, announced a series of measures aimed at reinforcing the integrity, inclusiveness, and sustainability of Nigeria\u2019s pension industry. Pension Boost 1.0 In a move that reshapes the retirement landscape, PenCom announced a landmark increase in monthly pensions from \u20a68.3 billion to \u20a611.9 billion, effective 1 June 2025. The initiative, powered by robust economic reforms and strong RSA fund performance, delivers a significant uplift for over 233,000 retirees under the Contributory Pension Scheme. This bold adjustment reflects the federal government\u2019s unwavering commitment to restoring dignity and financial stability for Nigeria\u2019s retired workforce. The Inaugural Pension Industry Leadership Retreat Held in May 2025, the Retreat set a bold agenda for the future of the industry, focusing on infrastructure financing, legislative engagement, investment diversification, and the rebranding of the Micro Pension Plan as \u2018The Personal Pension Plan\u2019, making it more accessible to Nigeria\u2019s growing informal sector. Pension Compliance Redefined In a landmark enforcement directive, PenCom announced that any entity, including service providers, parent firms, subsidiaries, and institutional shareholders that lacks a valid Pension Clearance Certificate (PCC) by November 2025 will be barred from doing business within Nigeria\u2019s pension ecosystem. This measure underscores that compliance is no longer optional. Erasing Accrued Pension Rights Arrears Thanks to the visionary support of President Bola Ahmed Tinubu, accrued pension payments have been made to retirees exiting service up to March 2025, drastically reducing a backlog that once stretched 21 months. PenCom is now poised to completely eliminate pension arrears, historic shift that guarantees timely benefits for future retirees. In making the announcements, the PenCom DG emphasized that, \u201cWe are building a pension system that is transparent, inclusive, and unshakable. One that delivers on its promise to every Nigerian worker. This is a new era of compliance, and trust, and it guarantees a brighter future for all retirees.\u201d PenCom in collaboration with FSD Africa Sensitization Workshop on Alternative Asset Investments The National Pension Commission (PenCom), in collaboration with Financial Sector Deepening Africa (FSD Africa), is charting a bold new path for pension fund growth and sustainability through a high-level Sensitization Workshop on Alternative Asset Investments. The landmark event, taking place from June 26\u201327, 2025 at Eko Hotel & Suites, Lagos, brings together Chairpersons of the Investment Strategy and Risk Management Committees of Pension Fund Administrators (PFAs), CEOs, regulators, and key capital market players. PenCom\u2019s Director General, Omolola Oloworaran, emphasized the fiduciary responsibility of investment committee leaders to think independently, act transparently, and embrace dynamic strategies that reflect today\u2019s economic realities. \u201cThis workshop is more than a knowledge-sharing platform\u2014it is a call to action,\u201d she stated. \u201cTo secure better outcomes for contributors, we must be bold, data-driven, and forward-looking in exploring alternative investment vehicles.\u201d In his remarks, British Deputy High Commissioner Jonny Baxter reiterated the United Kingdom\u2019s commitment to supporting Nigeria\u2019s transition to more strategic deployment of long-term capital. \u201cNigeria\u2019s pension industry represents a tremendous pool of long-term capital and stands as a testament to the country\u2019s progress,\u201d Baxter noted. \u201cHowever, a significant portion of these funds remain invested in conventional instruments, creating a compelling case for broader portfolio diversification into high-impact sectors.\u201d He emphasized that infrastructure, clean energy, and logistics are becoming increasingly attractive, underpinned by reforms and growing investor confidence\u2014offering promising avenues for long-term investment. Guillermo Larra\u00edn, a former chairman of the Chilean Pension Regulator, took center stage with a series of compelling case studies that drew from Chile\u2019s experience in deploying pension assets into infrastructure, real estate, and private equity. His sessions explored the regulatory consequences and practical realities of alternative investments, providing participants with tested frameworks and practical tools for implementation within the Nigerian context. This expertly curated list of speakers ensured that participants were not only informed but also inspired to rethink conventional investment strategies and embrace innovative, diversified, and risk-balanced approaches in line with global best practices. As Nigeria navigates global market volatility, this initiative signals a transformative step towards building a more resilient and future-proof pension industry\u2014one that not only safeguards retirement savings but also powers inclusive national development. Addendum to the Circular on Approval of Benefits Payments May 2025 Monthly Summary Fourth Quarter Report, 2024 The Revised Model State Law This is a Model Pension Law Designed for States of the Federation. April 2025 Monthly Summary Circular on Unethical Practice PenCom DG Meets Yobe Governor to Drive CPS Adoption in the State During the meeting, the DG briefed the Governor on the long-term benefits of CPS for state employees. Key discussion points included the passage of the CPS Bill, regular remittance of contributions, addressing past service liabilities through actuarial valuation, and setting up a Pension Bureau to manage the scheme effectively. Governor Buni raised concerns about inflation and currency depreciation affecting pension savings and expressed interest in measures to protect pension funds. He also emphasized the need for public awareness campaigns to educate stakeholders on the scheme\u2019s benefits and operations. The Governor assured the DG of his administration\u2019s readiness to institutionalize CPS by enacting a strong pension law, establishing required administrative structures, and working closely with PenCom. He also pledged support for pre-retirement training programs to prepare workers for life after service. Ms. Oloworaran praised the Governor\u2019s proactive stance, particularly his efforts to settle outstanding pension arrears, and promised ongoing technical support from PenCom to ensure a smooth rollout of the CPS in Yobe State. This strategic meeting signals significant progress toward establishing a secure and sustainable pension system for the workforce of Yobe State. Addendum to Circular on Compliance with Provisions of PRA Circular on Compliance with Provisions of PRA PenCom Requests LASG to Extend Periodic Pension Increase to CPS The Director General has urged the Lagos State Governor, Babajide Sanwo-Olu, to extend periodic pension increases currently enjoyed by Defined Benefit Scheme (DBS) retirees to those under the Contributory Pension Scheme (CPS) to boost their monthly pensions. The DG made the request during a recent courtesy visit to Governor Sanwo-Olu at the Lagos State Government House. The visit was to present the report of PenCom\u2019s 2024 routine inspection of the Lagos State Pension Commission (LASPEC). DG pointed out that currently, only DBS retirees are paid pension increases in Lagos State, leaving out their counterparts under the CPS. She made a case for the prioritisation of retirees under the CPS given the contributory nature of the scheme. She highlighted key achievements, including consistent deduction and remittance of employee contributions to their Retirement Savings Accounts (RSAs), full settlement of all backlog of accrued pension rights, the existence of a valid Group Life Insurance Policy for most public service employees, and the deployment of advanced ICT systems to enhance pension administration in Lagos State. In spite of this progress, the DG proposed a partnership with Lagos State to make the Pension Clearance Certificate (PCC) a mandatory requirement for companies seeking Lagos State Government contracts and services, as practiced at the federal level. In addition, the DG recommended transitioning unremitted contributions currently held in commercial banks\u2019 escrow accounts into Transitional Contribution Fund (TCF) accounts managed by Pension Fund Administrators (PFAs), to optimize investment returns. PenCom seeks amendment to Ondo Pension Law The DG visited the Ondo State Governor to discuss pension reforms aimed at enhancing retiree welfare and ensuring compliance with the Contributory Pension Scheme (CPS). She proposed amendments to the Ondo State Pension Law, including a Minimum Pension Guarantee (MPG) to protect vulnerable retirees and an Irrevocable Standing Payment Order (ISPO) to ensure consistent pension remittances across administrations. She also recommended making a Pension Compliance Certificate (PCC) mandatory for business licensing, contract awards, and registration renewals to boost employer accountability. She highlighted concerns from OSPEC\u2019s recent inspection, urging swift action to improve compliance. Aiyedatiwa assured PenCom that Ondo State would address most of these issues before the next inspection, reinforcing his administration\u2019s focus on retiree welfare and economic development through pension fund utilisation. March 2025 Monthly Summary Circular on Calculating and Reporting of Rate of Returns List of Organizations Issued Pension Clearance Certificates in 2025 Third Quarter Report, 2024 Expression of Interest (EoI)- Consultancy Services February 2025 Monthly Report January 2025 Monthly Report Circular on Approval and Payments of Benefits by PFAs Citizens Pensions Limited 3rd Floor, The Beacon Plot 15, Admiralty Way ,Lekki Phase 1 Lagos Press Release on the Engagement of Recovery Agents CERTIFIED LIST OF PRIMARY MORTGAGE BANKS AND COMMERCIAL BANKS Section 4.2.5 of the Guidelines on Accessing Retirement Savings Account (RSA) Balance Towards Payment of Equity Contribution for Residential Mortgage by RSA Holders (the Guidelines) stipulates that the Commission should liaise with the Central Bank of Nigeria on an annual basis to determine the mortgage lenders that meet the minimum requirements of the Guidelines and other conditions specified by the CBN. Section 4.2.6 of the Guidelines stipulates that the names of mortgage Lenders that meet the eligibility criteria of the Guidelines shall be published on the Commission\u2019s website on a bi-annual basis or when the CBN issues any new license. Consequent to the above, please download the list of approved licensed Primary Mortgage Banks and Commercial Banks as obtained from the CBN attached. December 2024 Monthly Report Website Privacy Notice Revised Circular on The Operations of Branch Offices and Service Centres by Licenced Pension Fund Administrators PenCom reshapes how Pension Fund Administrators (PFAs) run branch offices and service centres towards stronger presence and better service delivery to retirees and contributors Circular on Mandatory Provision of The Bank Verification Number (BVN) For RSA Registration and Data Recapture 2024 November Monthly Report Circular on The Revised Documentation for Retirement Savings Account (RSA) Registration List of Organizations Issued Pension Clearance Certificates in 2024 Change of Name: From Radix Pension Managers Limited to Cardinal Stone Pensions Limited Aisha Dahir Umar Dr. Umar Farouk Aminu Pencom Commends Governor Otti For His Commitment to Setting Up A Seamless Pension Scheme The National Pension Commission (PenCom) has commended the Governor of Abia State, Dr. Alex Otti, OFR, for his commitment to setup an effective and seamless pension scheme that will outlive his tenure as the State Governor. PENCOM gave the commendation on Tuesday, 3rd December, at the Umuehim Nvosi country home of the Governor when the DG of PenCom, Ms. Omolola Bridget-Oloworaran led some top officials of the Commission on a working visit to the Governor. During the visit, Ms. Bridget-Oloworaran commended the Governor for clearing the backlog of pension arrears that he met on assumption of office and signing the Abia State reform Pension Law 2024 passed by the State House of Assembly. In her words \u201cI am happy with the steps that Abia State has taken in passing the State Pension Reform Law 2024\u2033. Your Excellency, I want to thank you for these great steps (1) signing the law and (2) your commitment to wanting the CPS (Contributory Pension Scheme) to stay not just during your tenure but long after you have gone. \u201cAbia State is poised to become a mother of sustainable development for employee\u2019s welfare and I believe setting an example across the whole South East because as at today, there is no State in the South East that is fully compliant under the Contributory Pension Scheme. If Abia State moves fast, you be able to set an Example across the whole of the South East region and I dare say across the country in terms of some of the controls that you have put in place to ensure that this scheme not only survives your own tenure\u201d. She said that the Abia State pension Law was thoroughly worked on. She pointed out some areas that the State Government needed to look into towards establishing an effective and seamless pension scheme among which are employees\u2019 Biometric verification, working with Ministries, Departments, and Agencies (MDAs) to open or generate codes for employers, constituting the Pension Board, remittance of contributions to the CPS and including Pensioners in Health Insurance among others. Circular on Commercial Paper Circular on Implementation of Minimum Wage Guidance Note on Revised Programmed Withdrawal Template Version 2 Issuance of The Revised Programmed Withdrawal Template Version 2 2024 October Monthly Report 2024 September Monthly Report Dr. Charles E. Sylvester Emukowhate Mr. Clement Oyedele Akintola (RIP) Notice of Commencement of the 2024 Online Verification and Enrolment Exercise for Retirees/Prospective Retirees of Federal Government Treasury- Funded Ministries, Departments and Agencies (MDAS) Notice to All Employers: Introduction of the E-Pension Clearance Certificate Application 2024 August Monthly Report Customer Service Week HAPPY CUSTOMER SERVICE WEEK FROM ALL OF US @ PENCOM! Press Release: Merger Between ARM Pension Managers (PFA) Limited and Access Pensions Limited Invitation to Tender . .. \u2026 \u2026. Revised Documentation from RSA Holders for The Data Recapture Exercise \u2026.. Second Quarter Report, 2024 2024 July Monthly Report 2024 June Monthly Report 2024 May Monthly Report Review of Some Matters Relating to The Administration of Retirement and Terminal Benefits Under the Contributory Pension Scheme Circular on Emerging Matters in The Payment of Voluntary Contribution Under the Contributory Pension Scheme First Quarter Report, 2024 2024 April Monthly Report 2024 March Monthly Report Consumer Protection Framework for The Nigerian Pension Industry Fourth Quarter Report,2023 2024 February Monthly Report 2024 January Monthly Report REVISED GUIDELINES FOR RSA REGISTRATION REVISED REGULATION FOR THE TRANSFER OF RSAs THIRD QUARTER REPORT, 2023 2023 December Monthly Report 2023 November Monthly Report List of Organizations Issued Pension Compliance Certificates in 2023 2023 October Monthly Report REQUEST FOR EXPRESSION OF INTEREST REVISED CIRCULAR ON ENROLMENT OF FEDERAL GOVERNMENT EMPLOYEES Revised Guidelines for the Administration of Retirement Benefits of Professors and a Category of Politica