eting By sharing your interests and behavior as you visit our site, you increase the chance of seeing personalized content and offers. Save Accept all", "content_hash": "92320ad0b6304ac0b0f67266102c6ffd5f1a8f7c1b5e30c80ec6d0125f2ffd8f", "domain": "pencom.gov.ng", "fetched_at": "2026-10-09T05:18:21.077280+00:00", "language": ""}, {"id": "1141", "title": "Environment & Sustainability", "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/collections/environment/", "final_url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/collections/environment/", "text": "Environment & Sustainability | MIT OpenCourseWare | Free Online Course Materials menu search Give Now About OCW Help & Faqs Contact Us search GIVE NOW about ocw help & faqs contact us Environment & Sustainability Photo license: CC Zero (public domain) Like so many of the big challenges taken on at MIT, environmental and sustainability issues demand an interdisciplinary perspective. From declining fisheries to acute urban pollution to record-breaking global temperatures, the evidence of human impact on the environment continues to mount. And at the same time, the environment shapes us, as human society and institutions are built upon our connection to the weather, land, water, and other species. What can we learn from ecological systems and cycles? What solutions will allow people and the planet to thrive? MIT scholars, students and alumni are working to understand and help us make progress toward a more sustainable and just world. This core mission draws upon all of the fields represented at MIT: not just science, engineering, and technology, but also the humanities, arts, economics, history, architecture, urban planning, management, policy, and more. This OCW course collection is inspired by two interdisciplinary MIT programs. Many of the undergraduate courses fall within the undergraduate Environment and Sustainability Minor managed by MIT\u2019s Environmental Solutions Initiative (ESI); the OCW course list employs the undergraduate minor\u2019s four topic pillars. Many of the graduate-level courses are part of the MIT Sloan School of Management Sustainability Certificate curriculum. Also check out the MIT Climate Portal for many other Creative Commons-licensed open educational resources on climate change, including brief Explainer articles on key topics and the award-winning TILClimate podcast with educator guides. Featured Courses keyboard_arrow_left Previous Next keyboard_arrow_right RES.ENV-005 | Non-Credit Climate Science, Risk & Solutions: A Climate Primer Instructor: Prof. Kerry Emanuel Topics: Energy, Climate, Technology RES.ENV-007 | Non-Credit Geothermal Energy Networks: Transforming Our Thermal Energy System Instructor: MIT and HEET Topics: Energy, Renewables, Engineering 11.350 | Graduate Sustainable Real Estate Instructors: Prof. Siqi Zheng, Zhengzhen Tan, Prof. Juan Francisco Palacios Temprano Topics: Business, Real Estate, Energy 21W.775 | Undergraduate Writing about Nature and Environmental Issues Instructor: Dr. Cynthia Taft Topics: Humanities, History, Literature 11.165J | Undergraduate, Graduate Urban Energy Systems and Policy Instructor: Prof. David Hsu Topics: Energy, Social Science, Urban Studies RES.ENV-008 | Non-Credit Climate, Environment, and Sustainability Infusion Fellowship (CESIF) Instructor: Dr. Christopher Rabe Topics: Energy, Climate, Science CMS.631 | Undergraduate Data Storytelling Studio: Climate Change Instructor: Rahul Bhargava Topics: Fine Arts, Game Design, Media Studies 2.60J | Undergraduate Fundamentals of Advanced Energy Conversion Instructor: Prof. Ahmed F. Ghoniem Topics: Energy, Combustion, Fossil Fuels 11.601 | Graduate Introduction to Environmental Policy and Planning Instructor: Prof. Lawrence Susskind Topics: Social Science, Urban Studies, Public Administration RES.14-003 | Non-Credit The Roosevelt Project Instructor: The Roosevelt Project Team Topics: Energy, Climate, Social Science RES.EC-004 | Non-Credit Energy Needs Assessment Toolkit Instructor: Dr. Eric Verploegen Top